The approximate center used for the proof of concept is:

Latitude: 33.066° N
Longitude: 7.242° W

A publicly listed position for Ben Ahmed is approximately 33.06635° N and 7.24161° W.

The coordinates identify the approximate center of the study area, but a coordinate point alone does not define its spatial extent. The notebook will therefore use a defined buffer or bounding box around this point, depending on the coverage of the available multispectral data.

This spatial extent is an analytical proof-of-concept boundary. It should not be interpreted as an official administrative boundary for the city, municipality, province, or agricultural district.

Before processing, the selected study boundary will be checked against:

the multispectral scene footprint;
the available soil layers;
climate-data coverage;
crop-suitability layers;
agricultural land-cover data; and
any available field or parcel boundaries.
## 2.2 Agricultural Context

Agriculture is an important activity in the broader Casablanca-Settat region. The agricultural system includes crop production and livestock activities, with cereals, legumes, forage crops, vegetables, fruit trees, sheep, cattle, and dairy production contributing to the regional agricultural landscape.

The Ben Ahmed area is relevant for this proof of concept because farms located within the same broader climatic zone may still differ significantly in:

soil texture and soil properties;
water availability;
irrigation access;
field condition;
previous crops;
livestock ownership;
available pasture;
stored feed;
exposure to drought;
farm size; and
production objectives.

As a result, a single recommendation for the entire region would not adequately represent the conditions or priorities of individual farms.

The proposed system therefore combines regional environmental information with farmer-specific inputs. This makes it possible to generate recommendations that are locally informed while still considering the wider distribution of crops across neighboring farms.

##2.3 Climate and Water Constraints
The study area is characterized by water constraints, irregular rainfall, and exposure to seasonal or recurring periods of agricultural water stress.

These conditions make water compatibility a central component of crop planning. A crop should not be recommended only because it has a favorable general suitability score. Its water demand must also be compared with:

historical rainfall;
seasonal climatic conditions;
irrigation availability;
the reliability of the water source;
expected evapotranspiration;
the crop’s planting period;
the duration of its growing cycle;
its sensitivity to drought; and
its expected water deficit during the growing season.

The system therefore distinguishes between crops that are:

potentially suitable under rain-fed conditions;
suitable only with reliable irrigation;
relatively tolerant of water limitations;
sensitive to water stress; or
unsuitable under the farmer’s declared water conditions.

The proof of concept provides a relative water-compatibility assessment. It does not claim to replace field-level irrigation scheduling, hydraulic analysis, or direct measurements of water availability.

##2.4 Crop and Livestock Integration

The project treats crops and livestock as connected components of the same farming system.

A farmer who owns sheep or cattle may need to reserve part of the available land for:

forage production;
grazing;
cereal or legume crops with feed value;
dual-purpose crops;
hay or silage production; or
crops that produce usable residues.

For every synthetic farmer profile, the notebook may include inputs such as:

Plain Text
farm area
number of sheep
number of cattle
available pasture area
grazing availability
stored feed
purchased feed
available crop residues
irrigation availability
previous crop
farmer objective

Based on these inputs, the notebook estimates a feed gap:

Plain Text
Estimated feed need
- estimated grazing contribution
- stored or purchased feed
- usable crop residues
= estimated remaining feed gap

This calculation is used to determine whether forage or dual-purpose crops should receive additional weight in the recommendation.

The result represents an approximate feed-coverage indicator. It is not a complete animal ration and does not replace advice from a veterinarian or animal-nutrition specialist.

##2.5 Agricultural Challenges Addressed by the Proof of Concept

The Ben Ahmed proof of concept focuses on the following interconnected challenges:

Rainfall variability and drought exposure
Unequal or unreliable access to irrigation
Seasonal crop-water deficits
Differences in soil and field conditions
Repeated cultivation of the same crop or crop family
Insufficient crop rotation
Limited crop diversity across neighboring farms
Simultaneous demand for regional water resources
Livestock-feed shortages
Competition between food crops and feed crops for available land
Underuse of suitable crop residues and dual-purpose crops
Potential concentration of production in a limited number of crops
Market exposure when production is insufficiently diversified

These challenges motivate the integration of Earth observation, hyperspectral imagery, climate, soil, crop suitability, crop history, livestock needs, and regional crop-distribution information within a single decision-support framework.

Market data may be integrated in a future version, but the initial proof of concept does not attempt to forecast prices or guarantee financial returns.

## 2.6 Current Earth-Observation Baseline and Future Hyperspectral Extension

The current proof of concept uses Sentinel-2 Level-2A multispectral
imagery as its real Earth-observation baseline.

Sentinel-2 provides spatially consistent observations that can support:

- true-color visualization;
- relative vegetation-greenness characterization using NDVI;
- relative moisture-sensitive characterization using NDMI;
- cloud and cloud-shadow screening using the Scene Classification Layer;
- separation of selected broad surface-condition classes; and
- future multi-date comparison when comparable observations are added.

These outputs are indicators derived from satellite reflectance. They
must not be interpreted as direct measurements of crop health, soil
moisture, irrigation, crop yield, or nutrient deficiency.

A future Challenge 813 hyperspectral extension may provide narrower and
more detailed spectral information. Depending on its wavelength
coverage, spatial resolution, acquisition conditions, metadata, and
quality, it may support the extraction of red-edge, vegetation,
moisture-sensitive, exposed-soil, principal-component, or spectral
clustering features.

The future hyperspectral analysis will be compared against the current
Sentinel-2 baseline. Any increase in model complexity must be justified
by a measurable increase in useful information, interpretability, or
decision-support performance.

##2.7 Regional Diversification Approach

The proof of concept compares two recommendation approaches.

Independent recommendation

Each farmer profile is evaluated without considering the recommendations generated for neighboring farms.

This approach may identify the same highly ranked crop for many farms with similar environmental conditions.

Diversity-aware recommendation

Each farmer still receives environmentally and agronomically suitable alternatives, but the crop ranking also considers estimated regional crop concentration.

A crop that is already dominant may receive a concentration penalty, while a suitable but underrepresented crop may receive a diversification benefit.

The diversity mechanism does not force farmers to cultivate a particular crop. It adjusts the ranking only among crops that have already passed minimum environmental and agronomic suitability requirements.

The notebook may use indicators such as:

percentage of agricultural area assigned to each crop;
percentage assigned to each crop family;
dominant crop share;
number of crops represented;
Shannon diversity index; or
Herfindahl-Hirschman concentration index.

The objective is not maximum diversity under all circumstances. The objective is to avoid excessive concentration while maintaining crop suitability, water compatibility, livestock-feed coverage, and practical farm constraints.

##2.8 Proof-of-Concept Data Strategy

The notebook combines real datasets with synthetic farmer scenarios.

Real or externally derived data

The proof of concept aims to use real or established sources for:

Challenge 813 hyperspectral imagery;
study-area location;
climate indicators;
soil-property maps;
crop-suitability layers;
regional land-cover or crop-reference information;
crop water requirements; and
livestock-feed characteristics.
Synthetic scenario data

Synthetic data are used only for farm-specific information that cannot reliably be inferred from satellite imagery, including:

farm size;
exact farm location, if verified parcel boundaries are unavailable;
number of sheep;
number of cattle;
available pasture;
stored or purchased feed;
irrigation access;
previous crop;
farmer preferences; and
production objectives.

The synthetic records do not represent real farms, real farmers, or verified individual decisions. They are used to test whether the recommendation pipeline responds logically when the input conditions change.

For example, the system should produce different recommendations for:

a rain-fed farm without livestock;
a farm with sheep and limited pasture;
a farm with cattle and stored feed;
a farm with irrigation access;
a farm that previously cultivated a cereal; and
a farm located in a zone where one crop is already dominant.
##2.9 Proof-of-Concept Spatial and Analytical Scope

The initial proof of concept is intentionally limited and controlled.

The current baseline includes:

- 1 study area: Ben Ahmed;
- 1 explicitly defined analytical boundary;
- 2 Sentinel-2 Level-2A observation;
- Sentinel-2 quality screening using the Scene Classification Layer;
- 1 true-color composite;
- cloud-masked NDVI and NDMI indicators;
- a planned second Sentinel-2 date for temporal comparison;
- 12 synthetic farmer scenarios;
- 6 to 8 candidate crops;
- 2 livestock categories: sheep and cattle;
- 1 planning season;
- 1 previous-crop value per farmer scenario;
- 1 approximate livestock-feed coverage calculation;
- 1 transparent crop-suitability and water-compatibility assessment; and
- 1 comparison between independent and diversity-aware recommendations.

The following element is reserved for a later project stage:

- Challenge 813 hyperspectral inspection, preprocessing, feature
  extraction, and comparison with the Sentinel-2 baseline.
The project does not attempt to produce:

a complete agricultural model for all of Morocco;
an exact crop-price forecast;
a veterinary feeding prescription;
a guaranteed yield prediction;
a complete multi-year crop-rotation schedule;
confirmed parcel-level soil chemistry;
exact identification of every crop from one satellite acquisition; or
an operational farm-management platform.

Limiting the scope makes it possible to demonstrate the full analytical workflow transparently and reproducibly within one notebook.

##2.10 Study-Area Objective

The objective of the Ben Ahmed proof of concept is to test whether a diversity-aware agricultural recommendation system can identify suitable crop alternatives for individual farmer scenarios while also considering:

local environmental conditions;
available water;
remotely sensed land condition;
previous crop and crop-family rotation;
livestock-feed requirements;
land competition between food and feed crops; and
crop concentration across the study area.

Rather than imposing a single crop recommendation, the system provides several suitable alternatives and proposes a diversified allocation of the available farm area.

The final output may allocate land among categories such as:

food crops;
forage crops;
dual-purpose crops;
legumes;
soil-improving crops; and
alternative crops that contribute to regional diversification.

The final decision remains with the farmer. The notebook provides the supporting data, scoring logic, assumptions, and explanations needed to make that decision more informed.

The intended result is a transparent prototype demonstrating how Earth observation and established agricultural data can be translated into practical, farmer-centered decision support.


