### Comparison Interpretation and Limitations
The two-date comparison was conducted using 2,153,747 pixels that passed the relevant quality screening on both acquisition dates. These pixels represent approximately 96.65% of the analytical study area for both NDVI and NDMI, providing broad and spatially consistent coverage for the comparison.

Across the common valid pixels, mean NDVI increased from 0.1646 on 18 December 2025 to 0.4693 on 16 March 2026, corresponding to a mean increase of 0.3047. Median NDVI increased from 0.1454 to 0.4844, with a difference of 0.3390. The similarity between the mean and median results indicates that the higher March NDVI was spatially widespread rather than being produced only by a small number of unusually high-value pixels.

The NDVI maps support this statistical result. Much of the agricultural landscape had relatively low NDVI values in December, while substantially higher values were observed across many fields in March. The NDVI difference map is predominantly positive, although some localized areas show limited or negative differences. These patterns indicate that the near-infrared and red reflectance relationship associated with vegetation activity differed substantially between the two observations.

Mean NDMI increased from -0.0879 in December to 0.1909 in March, corresponding to a mean difference of 0.2788. Median NDMI increased from -0.1036 to 0.1969, with a difference of 0.3005. The predominantly positive NDMI difference map indicates that the relative moisture-sensitive spectral response was higher across most common valid pixels in March than in December.

The standard deviation of NDVI was 0.0720 in December and 0.1220 in March, while the standard deviation of NDMI increased from 0.0683 to 0.1157. These results indicate greater spatial variability in both indices during the March observation. In other words, differences among the observed fields and other land surfaces were more pronounced in March than in December.

The true-color images are consistent with the index results. The December observation contains more brown and exposed-surface tones, whereas the March observation contains more green-toned fields. Built-up areas, roads, and other non-vegetated surfaces remain visually distinct in both images.

The observed differences are compatible with a seasonal change in land and vegetation conditions between December and March. However, the comparison does not establish the cause of that change. The differences may reflect a combination of vegetation development, crop calendars, planting or harvesting activity, rainfall, differences in exposed-soil conditions, field management, atmospheric effects, or other seasonal factors.

NDVI is treated only as a relative vegetation indicator, while NDMI is treated as a relative moisture-sensitive spectral indicator. Higher NDVI does not independently prove healthier crops, increased yield, or improved agricultural conditions. Similarly, higher NDMI does not directly measure soil moisture, irrigation volume, rainfall, crop water availability, or water stress.

The analysis contains only two acquisition dates and therefore represents a descriptive two-date comparison rather than a temporal trend analysis. It does not identify crop species, distinguish agricultural vegetation from every other type of vegetation, diagnose crop stress, confirm irrigation activity, or determine the causes of the observed spectral differences. Stronger interpretation would require additional comparable acquisition dates together with rainfall, temperature, crop-calendar, field-boundary, soil, irrigation, and field-observation information.
#  **6. Future Challenge 813 Hyperspectral Integration**
## 6.1 Current Status

A suitable Challenge 813 hyperspectral scene covering the Ben Ahmed
analytical study area is not used in the current implementation.

This section defines the processing and validation procedure that will be
applied when suitable hyperspectral coverage becomes available. It is a
future integration specification, not a completed result.
### 6.2 Planned data-ingestion checks

1. Locate the Challenge 813 file.
2. Identify its sensor and product level.
3. Inspect variables, dimensions, and storage format.
4. Read wavelength and full-width-at-half-maximum metadata.
5. identify spatial and spectral axes.
6. Verify the coordinate reference system.
7. extract the scene footprint.
8. Test geographic overlap with Ben Ahmed.
9. Inspect acquisition date and observation conditions.
10. Locate quality flags and no-data definitions.
11. Identify restricted or redistributable components.
12. Record sensor-specific preprocessing assumptions.
# 7. Planned Hyperspectral Feature Extraction and Baseline Comparison
The future hyperspectral stage will not treat all bands as ordinary
image channels.

Before analysis, invalid, atmospheric-absorption, edge, or otherwise
noisy bands will be removed using sensor metadata and documented quality
criteria. Reflectance values will be standardized only after valid-band
selection.

The following features will be considered only when supported by the
available wavelength coverage:

- narrow-band vegetation indicators;
- red-edge features;
- moisture-sensitive features;
- exposed-soil features;
- spectral principal components; and
- simple spectral clustering.

The hyperspectral outputs will be compared with the Sentinel-2 baseline.
The comparison will determine whether the additional bands provide a
defensible improvement over simpler multispectral indicators.  

Any land-condition classes produced from spectral features will be
described as screening categories, for example:

- relatively high vegetation activity;
- relatively low vegetation activity;
- exposed-surface candidate;
- non-agricultural surface;
- mixed or uncertain surface; and
- invalid or low-quality observation.

These classes will not be interpreted as confirmed crop stress, disease,
nutrient deficiency, irrigation failure, or soil degradation without
independent reference information.
