The NASA POWER climate record for 2001–2025 shows a pronounced seasonal cycle in the Ben Ahmed study area. Mean annual precipitation was approximately 437 mm, although rainfall was unevenly distributed throughout the year. Approximately 76% of the mean annual precipitation occurred between October and March. November recorded the highest average monthly precipitation, followed by March and December. In contrast, June, July, and August were comparatively dry and together accounted for only about 3% of the mean annual precipitation.

Mean monthly air temperature increased from 11.21 °C in January to 26.91 °C in August. The monthly maximum-temperature indicator reached its highest average value in July, while the monthly minimum-temperature indicator reached its lowest average value in January. These results describe the regional seasonal temperature cycle but do not independently confirm heat stress, cold stress, or reduced crop performance.

Average relative humidity was highest during winter, exceeding 70% from December to March, and lowest during July and August at approximately 52%. Modelled root-zone soil wetness followed a broadly similar seasonal pattern. The highest average wetness values occurred during winter and early spring, while the lowest values occurred during July and August. Surface solar irradiance displayed the opposite pattern, increasing from winter toward summer and reaching its highest average levels during June and July.

Together, the variables describe a transition from relatively cooler, wetter, more humid, and lower-radiation conditions during winter to warmer, drier, less humid, and higher-radiation conditions during summer. The climate profile will be used as regional environmental context when evaluating crop suitability and generating subsequent recommendations.

The NASA POWER observations have a substantially coarser spatial resolution than the Sentinel-2 imagery. They are therefore not aligned with individual Sentinel-2 pixels and are not interpreted as parcel-level measurements. The same regional climate profile is applied to the synthetic farmer scenarios. In addition, the root-zone wetness variable is a modelled regional indicator and does not replace field soil-moisture measurements, irrigation records, or soil-property data.

These results alone do not confirm crop stress, irrigation failure, disease, nutrient deficiency, soil degradation, or expected yield. Such conclusions would require crop-specific thresholds, planting and phenological information, field observations, irrigation data, soil measurements, and independent reference information.

