### Soil Results and Interpretation  

SoilGrids median predictions were used to characterize the regional soil conditions around the Ben Ahmed study area. Five properties were evaluated: soil pH in water, clay content, sand content, silt content, and soil organic carbon. Predictions were obtained for the 0–5 cm, 5–15 cm, and 15–30 cm depth intervals. The depth-specific regional statistics were then combined using thickness-weighted aggregation to represent the upper 0–30 cm soil profile.

All 15 expected property-depth layers were downloaded and processed successfully. Each layer contained 8,852 valid pixels, allowing the properties and depth intervals to be compared over a common regional extent.

Soil pH
The depth-weighted regional soil pH was approximately 7.46, with a weighted median of 7.48. The depth-specific regional mean increased from 7.11 in the 0–5 cm layer to 7.51 in the 5–15 cm layer and 7.55 in the 15–30 cm layer.

These modelled values indicate approximately neutral to mildly alkaline conditions across the regional 0–30 cm profile. The increase with depth suggests that the subsurface predictions are slightly more alkaline than the immediate surface layer. However, these differences represent regional model outputs and should not be interpreted as measured pH profiles for individual fields.

The pH result alone does not determine nutrient availability, amendment requirements, crop performance, or fertilizer needs. Such decisions would require representative field sampling and laboratory analysis.

Soil Texture
The regional depth-weighted texture estimates were approximately:

30.74% clay;
39.82% sand; and
19.08% silt.
Among the three reported components, sand had the largest regional weighted mean, followed by clay and silt. The results therefore indicate the simultaneous presence of substantial coarse and fine mineral fractions, rather than a regional profile dominated exclusively by either sand or clay.

The depth-specific predictions also show vertical differences. Mean clay content increased from approximately 28.20% at 0–5 cm to 29.33% at 5–15 cm and 32.53% at 15–30 cm. In contrast, mean sand content decreased slightly from 40.58% at the surface to 39.42% at 15–30 cm. Mean silt content decreased from 20.92% at 0–5 cm to 17.40% at 15–30 cm. Within the modelled regional profile, the deeper layer therefore contains a larger clay contribution and smaller sand and silt contributions than the surface layer.

The three depth-weighted regional texture estimates sum to approximately 89.64%, rather than 100%. Common-pixel quality checks produced similar mean texture totals of approximately 89.71%, 90.05%, and 89.34% for the three respective depth intervals. This shows that the difference is not caused simply by using different valid-pixel masks.

The original SoilGrids-derived values were retained without silently forcing the texture components to sum to 100%. Because of this consistency limitation, a definitive soil-texture class was not assigned. If a later crop-suitability method requires texture fractions summing to 100%, a separate normalized texture dataset may be created, but it must be clearly labelled as a derived transformation rather than an original SoilGrids prediction.

Soil Organic Carbon
The depth-weighted regional soil organic-carbon estimate was approximately 11.28 g/kg, with a weighted median of 11.37 g/kg. This is numerically equivalent to approximately 1.13% soil organic carbon by mass.

The depth-specific predictions show a clear decrease with depth. Mean soil organic carbon declined from approximately 16.13 g/kg in the 0–5 cm layer to 11.69 g/kg in the 5–15 cm layer and 9.39 g/kg in the 15–30 cm layer. The model therefore predicts a greater concentration of soil organic carbon near the surface and lower concentrations in the deeper part of the 0–30 cm profile.

This vertical pattern is relevant to the regional characterization, but the estimated organic-carbon values do not independently establish soil fertility, organic-matter management requirements, nutrient availability, soil degradation, or expected crop yield.

Spatial Variation and Interpretation Limits
The depth-specific minimum, maximum, median, and standard-deviation values indicate that the soil properties are not spatially uniform across the regional extraction area. For example, the 15–30 cm clay predictions range from approximately 21.8% to 41.9%, while the surface sand predictions range from approximately 33.6% to 60.2%. The weighted regional means should therefore be treated as summaries of varying modelled conditions rather than values that apply identically throughout the study area.

All results are median SoilGrids model predictions at approximately 250 m native resolution. They are not laboratory measurements and are not interpreted as confirmed soil properties for individual farms, synthetic farmer points, agricultural parcels, or Sentinel-2 pixels. The soil extraction area also extends slightly beyond the analytical Sentinel-2 boundary and is used as a regional environmental context.

Resampling the soil layers onto a 10 m Sentinel-2 grid would support computational alignment but would not increase their native informational resolution. Parcel-specific soil classification and management decisions would require representative sampling, laboratory analysis, verified field boundaries, and information about crop and management history.

Overall, the SoilGrids results describe a regional upper-soil profile with approximately neutral to mildly alkaline modelled conditions, substantial sand and clay contributions, increasing clay content with depth, and decreasing soil organic carbon with depth. These properties provide useful regional context for subsequent crop-suitability screening, but they are not sufficient on their own to prescribe crops, fertilizers, amendments, or other field-management actions.
