### 8.1.4 Data Alignment and Normalization

The environmental datasets used in this prototype have different spatial, temporal, and numerical characteristics. Sentinel-2 provides 10 m Earth-observation indicators, SoilGrids provides modelled soil properties at approximately 250 m native resolution, GAEZ provides coarse regional crop-suitability classes, and NASA POWER provides a regional climate time series for the representative study-area location.

The datasets were therefore not treated as if they represented observations at the same spatial resolution. Resampling a coarse dataset onto the Sentinel-2 grid would increase the number of pixels but would not create new environmental information.

For the current proof of concept:

NASA POWER variables were summarized as regional climate indicators for 2001–2025.
SoilGrids properties were summarized over the upper 0–30 cm profile using depth-weighted regional means.
GAEZ suitability classes were converted to a transparent screening score between 0 and 1 for each supported crop.
Sentinel-2 NDVI and NDMI were retained as separate remotely sensed land-condition indicators.
Crop names and numerical formats were standardized before integration.
Comparable scoring variables were restricted to the range from 0 to 1.
Original physical variables such as rainfall, temperature, pH, soil organic carbon, and soil-texture fractions were retained in their original units and were not replaced by normalized values.
The aligned outputs are regional screening inputs. They are not parcel-level measurements and do not imply that climate, soil, GAEZ, and Sentinel-2 have the same native resolution or uncertainty.
