## 12.5 and 13.6 Results Summary and Limitations

The independent and diversity-aware approaches use the same eligibility rules, component weights, feed-gap information, and land-allocation constraints. Their key difference is the treatment of the diversity component. Independent scoring uses a neutral value for every crop, while diversity-aware scoring rewards crops and crop families that occupy smaller shares in allocations already made.

A successful diversity-aware result would generally show a lower dominant crop share and HHI, together with a higher Shannon diversity and effective number of crops. These changes must be interpreted jointly because one indicator alone does not fully describe the allocation pattern. The crop-share comparison identifies which crops gained or lost area when the diversity mechanism was activated.

The allocation procedure respects crop-level maximum fractions and allocates the full area of each synthetic farm. Feed-oriented crops receive a moderate allocation-weight boost when the farmer has a larger indicative feed-area requirement. This boost influences allocation among eligible crops but does not constitute a nutritional or forage-production guarantee.

The analysis remains a prototype. Candidate-crop attributes, scoring weights, thresholds, feed coefficients, and allocation limits include assumptions that require calibration. Environmental suitability is regional rather than parcel-specific, four crop suitability values remain provisional assumptions, and the diversity-aware method is sequential and sensitive to farmer processing order. The outputs should therefore be interpreted as transparent scenario comparisons rather than farm prescriptions or estimates of actual regional crop production.

