# **14. Results**
## 14.1 Overall Results
The prototype successfully generated crop rankings and land-allocation recommendations for all twelve synthetic farmer scenarios. Ninety-six farmer-crop combinations were evaluated, representing twelve farmer scenarios and eight candidate crops. Eligibility screening excluded crops that required unavailable irrigation or had aligned environmental suitability below the prototype threshold of 0.40.

Each farmer retained at least three eligible crops. Both the independent and diversity-aware allocation approaches assigned exactly 100% of each synthetic farm area while respecting crop-specific maximum allocation fractions. The total allocated area was 121.5 hectares under each approach.

Six crops received land in the final allocations: wheat, barley, lentil, faba bean, forage oats, and alfalfa. Chickpea and maize were excluded because their aligned environmental suitability scores were 0.000 under the selected historical, rain-fed, low-input GAEZ scenario. These values are scenario-specific and do not establish that the crops are unsuitable under every possible production system.

The final recommendations varied according to environmental suitability, irrigation availability, water compatibility, previous crop family, livestock-feed needs, farmer objectives, soil-health contribution, and regional crop concentration. This confirms that the recommendation engine responds to differences among the synthetic farm scenarios rather than producing one identical recommendation for every farmer.
## 14.2 Scenario Without Livestock
Farmer F01 represents a four-hectare rain-fed farm without livestock. Its primary objective is household-food production, and its previous crop was wheat, which belongs to the cereal family. Because no livestock are present, the feed-contribution component receives a neutral score and the recommendation is driven mainly by environmental suitability, water compatibility, household-food contribution, and crop rotation.

Under independent scoring, lentil ranked first with a final score of 0.708, followed by faba bean at 0.670 and wheat at 0.618. The recommended independent allocation assigned approximately 23.17% of the farm to lentil, 21.91% to faba bean, 20.21% to wheat, 18.98% to barley, and 15.73% to forage oats.

Lentil and faba bean ranked highly because the previous crop was a cereal, meaning that legume alternatives received a strong rotation score. Wheat remained competitive because it had the highest externally supported environmental-suitability score, but repeating the cereal family reduced its rotation contribution.

The allocation demonstrates that a household-food objective does not necessarily result in a single-crop recommendation. The system retains cereal food production while introducing legumes and an additional cereal-forage option to support rotation and diversification.
