Farmer F03 represents a ten-hectare rain-fed farm with twenty sheep, 1.5 hectares of pasture, and limited stored, purchased, and residue-based feed resources. The previous crop was wheat, and the primary objective is balanced food and feed production.

The feed module estimated an annual livestock-feed requirement of 11.00 tonnes and an available feed contribution of approximately 3.60 tonnes. The resulting estimated feed gap was approximately 7.41 tonnes, corresponding to a feed-gap ratio of 0.673 and a high feed-gap category. The indicative minimum forage area was approximately 1.85 hectares, or 18.5% of the farm.

Under independent scoring, faba bean ranked first with a final score of 0.635, followed by lentil at 0.627 and barley at 0.586. The recommended allocation assigned approximately 22.92% to faba bean, 21.17% to barley, 19.84% to forage oats, 19.09% to lentil, and 17.00% to wheat.

The recommendation balances several competing needs. Faba bean and lentil contribute to crop-family rotation following a cereal, barley provides both food and feed value, forage oats respond directly to the livestock-feed constraint, and wheat maintains a household-food component. However, the allocation does not claim to eliminate the full feed gap because forage yield and animal requirements are represented using prototype coefficients.
