## 14.4 Scenario With Cattle
Farmer F05 represents a twelve-hectare farm with moderate irrigation reliability and six cattle. The farm contains two hectares of pasture and has stored feed, purchased feed, and existing crop residues. Its primary objective is cattle-feed production, while its secondary objective is maintaining food production.

The feed module estimated an annual feed requirement of 21.00 tonnes and approximately 8.93 tonnes of available feed. The remaining feed gap was approximately 12.08 tonnes, corresponding to a high feed-gap category. The indicative minimum forage area was approximately 3.02 hectares, equivalent to about 25.2% of the farm.

Under independent scoring, alfalfa ranked first with a score of 0.737, followed by faba bean at 0.654 and forage oats at 0.634. Alfalfa became eligible because irrigation was available and ranked strongly due to its direct feed contribution and soil-health value. The independent allocation assigned approximately 23.88% to alfalfa, 21.19% to faba bean, 20.55% to forage oats, 19.12% to barley, and 15.26% to wheat.

This result shows that the model increases the importance of forage and dual-purpose crops when cattle demand creates a substantial feed constraint. Wheat and barley remain in the allocation to avoid assigning the complete farm to specialized forage production and to preserve food, residue, and diversification functions.
## 14.5 Scenario With Limited Irrigation
Farmer F08 represents a nine-hectare farm with limited irrigation reliability, thirty sheep, two cattle, and a livestock-feed production objective. Its available pasture and reported feed resources provide only partial coverage of the estimated livestock demand.

The module estimated an annual feed demand of 23.50 tonnes and approximately 3.58 tonnes of available feed. The resulting feed gap was approximately 19.92 tonnes, representing a feed-gap ratio of 0.848 and a very high feed-gap category. The indicative minimum forage area was approximately 4.98 hectares, or 55.3% of the total farm area.

Under independent scoring, alfalfa ranked first with a score of 0.713, followed by faba bean at 0.697 and forage oats at 0.683. The recommended independent allocation assigned approximately 22.63% to alfalfa, 22.12% to faba bean, 21.66% to forage oats, 20.22% to barley, and 13.38% to wheat.

The recommendation favors forage and dual-purpose crops but does not allocate the entire indicative feed-area requirement directly. This occurs because the allocation procedure balances feed contribution with environmental suitability, crop-allocation limits, food production, rotation, and diversification. In practice, a farm with such a large estimated deficit could also require additional purchased feed, improved forage productivity, adjustment of livestock numbers, or more reliable irrigation.
## 14.6 Independent Versus Diversity-Aware Comparison
The independent and diversity-aware approaches both allocated the complete 121.5-hectare synthetic farm area and represented six crops. The independent approach produced a Shannon diversity index of 1.7410, an effective number of crops of 5.7033, and an HHI concentration value of 0.1814. The diversity-aware approach produced a slightly higher Shannon index of 1.7426 and effective number of crops of 5.7122, together with a slightly lower HHI of 0.1809.

The diversity-aware method therefore produced a small overall improvement in regional diversity. The cereal-family share decreased from approximately 57.47% to 57.05%, while the forage-legume share increased from approximately 13.91% to 14.22%. Alfalfa recorded the largest positive crop-share change, increasing by approximately 0.31 percentage points.

However, the dominant crop remained faba bean, and its share increased marginally from 21.49% to 21.54%. The result shows that the diversity mechanism changed regional allocation only slightly. This is partly because the diversity component represented 10% of the final score, while environmental suitability, water compatibility, farmer objectives, livestock-feed constraints, and allocation limits remained influential.

The diversity-aware method should therefore be interpreted as a moderate ranking adjustment rather than a mechanism that forces equal crop shares. The small improvement is nevertheless consistent with its purpose: encouraging suitable underrepresented alternatives without overriding environmental and farm-level constraints.
