## 14.7 Main Results and Interpretation
The scenario comparison confirms that the recommendation engine responds logically to different farmer constraints. Farms without livestock emphasize household-food production, water compatibility, and crop rotation. Farms with sheep or cattle assign greater importance to forage and dual-purpose crops, particularly when the estimated feed gap is high. Irrigation availability controls whether irrigation-dependent crops such as alfalfa can enter the eligible crop set.

Across the regional prototype, the diversity-aware mechanism produced a small reduction in overall concentration while preserving complete farm-area allocation and crop eligibility. The limited magnitude of the change reflects the intentionally moderate diversity weight and the strong influence of other agronomic and farmer-specific components.

These results demonstrate the behavior of a transparent prototype rather than establishing optimal crop plans for real farms. Four of the eight crops use provisional environmental-suitability assumptions, and many other crop attributes and livestock coefficients remain assumption-based. The recommendations must therefore be interpreted as scenario-screening outputs requiring local validation.

